## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical E2E testing skills around feature flags, data seeding, cross-device validation, and deterministic mocks. It also evaluates how candidates handle paralellization, event deduplication, and rollback correctness in a live-like rollout.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and gradual rollouts
- Deterministic data seeding for stable tests
- Mocking external services (flag service, analytics)
- Cross-device (desktop/mobile) parity and isolation
- Event deduplication and correct rollback behavior

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright skeleton for ranking_v2 on/off
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('ranking_v2 enabled shows new ranking and analytics', async ({ page, request }) => {
  // seed data and mock flag service to return true for ranking_v2
  // navigate to search and verify ordering matches ranking_v2
  // assert analytics endpoint received a single event per click
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate rollout skew and handle hotfix rollbacks?
- How would you scale this test to 1000+ products with performance constraints?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical E2E skills: gating UX with feature flags, deterministic tests, data seeding, and test isolation using Cypress or Playwright.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and gating
- Test data seeding per run
- LocalStorage state as idempotence marker
- Network/mocks to deterministically simulate flag responses
- Time-bounded execution for beginner scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright skeleton
test('onboarding respects flag', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.route('**/flags**', r => r.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ onboarding: true }) }));
  await page.goto('https://app.local/');
  await expect(page.locator('#onboard-modal')).toBeVisible();
  await page.locator('#finish').click();
  const stored = await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.getItem('onboarding_complete'));
  expect(stored).toBe('true');
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('#onboard-modal')).toBeHidden();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to multi-tenant setups with per-tenant flag overrides?
- How would you ensure the test remains reliable when the flag toggles during a session?

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses a candidate's ability to design comprehensive E2E tests across multiple regions, handle feature-flag driven data visibility with proper access control, and demonstrate resilience testing in a distributed microservices architecture.

## Key Concepts

- **Multi-region data seeding**: Populating NA, EU, and APAC regions with identical baseline transactions to enable deterministic cross-region reconciliation
- **Eventual consistency**: Simulating regional latency and failures to verify the system handles asynchronous data propagation correctly
- **Feature flags with RBAC**: Toggling PII visibility based on user roles, with proper rollback behavior when flags change
- **Deterministic verification**: Using mocks and seeds to create repeatable test conditions
- **Observability**: Ensuring proper logging and monitoring during flag state transitions

## Code Example

```javascript
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

const REGIONS = ['NA', 'EU', 'APAC'];

test.describe('Financial Reporting Portal E2E', () => {
  test.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {
    await page.goto('/auth/login');
  });

  test('should reconcile data across regions with eventual consistency', async ({ page }) => {
    // Seed identical transactions across all regions
    await seedIdenticalTransactions(REGIONS);

    // Inject regional latency to simulate eventual consistency
    await mockService.delay('NA', 5000);
    await mockService.delay('EU', 3000);
    await mockService.delay('APAC', 8000);

    // Navigate to dashboard and verify data reconciliation
    await page.click('[data-testid="refresh-data"]');
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="total-revenue"]')).toHaveText('$300,000');
  });

  test('should hide PII for non-admin users via feature flag', async ({ page }) => {
    // Set feature flag to hide PII
    await featureFlag.set('show-pii', false);

    await page.loginAs('non-admin');
    await page.goto('/reports');

    // Verify PII is masked
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="customer-ssn"]')).toHaveText('***-**-1234');

    // Verify rollback behavior when flag changes
    await featureFlag.set('show-pii', true);
    await page.reload();
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="customer-ssn"]')).toHaveText('123-45-6789');
  });

  test('should handle regional outage gracefully', async ({ page }) => {
    // Simulate EU region outage
    await mockService.fail('EU', 503);

    await page.goto('/reports');
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="eu-status"]')).toContainText('Region unavailable');

    // Verify other regions still function
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="na-revenue"]')).toBeVisible();

    // Restore EU and verify reconciliation
    await mockService.restore('EU');
    await page.click('[data-testid="reconcile"]');
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="eu-status"]')).toContainText('Synced');
  });

  test('should enforce role-based access control', async ({ page }) => {
    await page.loginAs('admin');
    await page.goto('/reports');
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="admin-panel"]')).toBeVisible();

    await page.loginAs('viewer');
    await page.goto('/reports');
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="admin-panel"]')).not.toBeVisible();
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests at the beginner level often miss realistic offline scenarios and deterministic retries. This question probes practical E2E thinking: isolation, network control, and idempotent submission.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end orchestration of multi-step flows
- Auto-save timing and state persistence (e.g., localStorage/IndexedDB)
- Offline/online transition handling and reconciling state
- Mocking external APIs (identity) for determinism
- Ensuring single, idempotent final submit

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright sketch (context-level offline, data isolation, mocked API)
const context = await browser.newContext({ storageState: 'test-user.json' });
const page = await context.newPage();
await context.setOffline(true);
await page.goto('https://finapp/onboarding');
// perform step interactions, wait for auto-save marker, then go online
await context.setOffline(false);
// verify single submit
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to mobile vs desktop layouts?
- How would you validate that auto-save writes to the correct storage (localStorage vs IndexedDB) across browsers?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for real-time collaboration require multi-user concurrency, deterministic replay of complex conflict resolution, and presence signaling. This question probes the ability to design an end-to-end scenario that exercises OT, WebSocket events, and race-condition handling in a realistic setting.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end multi-user collaboration
- Deterministic OT mock to ensure repeatable runs
- WebSocket presence and real-time cursor synchronization
- Test data isolation and idempotent retries
- Tooling trade-offs between Playwright and Cypress

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch (Playwright):
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('collab editor concurrent edits with OT', async ({ browser }) => {
  const ctx1 = await browser.newContext();
  const page1 = await ctx1.newPage();
  const ctx2 = await browser.newContext();
  const page2 = await ctx2.newPage();
  await page1.goto('/doc/abc');
  await page2.goto('/doc/abc');
  // mock OT engine to deterministic edits
  // trigger concurrent edits and wait for batch window
  // assert final content matches OT-applied result
  // verify presence cursors updated in real time
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate network degradation or WebSocket partitions?
- How would you validate idempotent retries in case of transient OT conflicts?

## Why This Is Asked
This checks understanding of test isolation and determinism in E2E tests using mocks for external services.

## Key Concepts
- Data seeding and reset for repeatable tests
- Network interception and mocked APIs
- End-to-end flow verification and backend validation
- CI reliability and speed trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright test sketch
test('checkout end-to-end', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.route('**/payments', route => route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ status:'success', id:'PAY123' }) }));
  await page.request.post('https://test.api/reset-db', { data: { seed: 'default' } });
  await page.goto('https://shop.example.com/product/1');
  await page.click('text=Add to cart');
  await page.goto('https://shop.example.com/checkout');
  await page.fill('#name', 'Test User');
  await page.fill('#address', '123 Main St');
  await page.click('text=Pay');
  await page.locator('text=Order Confirmed').waitFor();
  // backend verification
  const res = await page.request.get('https://test.api/orders/123');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this test for a flaky payment API?
- What metrics would you collect to monitor CI stability for this flow?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a complex, cross-origin integration scenario with iframe messaging, localStorage persistence, and multi-tenant isolation—common in partner ecosystems like Salesforce, Cloudflare, and Coinbase. It probes reliability under network blips and ensures data integrity across domains.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-origin messaging via postMessage between host and iframe
- Frame targeting with frameLocator/selectors
- Draft persistence in localStorage and autosave recovery
- Network fault injection and retry semantics
- Tenant data isolation across partner domains

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright sketch (conceptual only)
const host = page.locator('#host-app');
const frame = host.frameLocator('iframe#widget-form');
await frame.locator('input[name="name"]').fill('Acme Corp');
// simulate cross-origin event
await page.evaluate(() => {
  window.postMessage({type: 'INIT', tenant: 'tenantA'}, '*');
});
// flaky submit
await page.route('https://api.partner.com/submit', route => {
  route.fulfill({ status: 500, body: '{}' });
});
await frame.locator('button[type="submit"]').click();
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test race conditions between postMessage handlers across both domains?
- How would you validate that autosaved drafts don’t leak between tenants when the host cache is cleared?

